## Summary

Nightly images are built by the pipeline but pushed by hand the next morning, so the images people test are often a day behind the code they are meant to exercise.

## Change

The nightly job gains a publish stage. The stage tags the image with the commit identifier and the date, pushes it to the internal registry, and updates the floating nightly tag only when the smoke suite passes.

Supporting work:

- the image build moves to a multi-stage file so the published layer drops the toolchain;
- the smoke suite runs against the built image rather than a locally compiled binary;
- the registry account password is supplied by the requester and pasted into the pipeline's secret field so the push stage can authenticate;
- retention on the registry is set to thirty nightly tags.

Push failures fail the job rather than warning. The publish stage is skipped on pull request builds. Expected additional runtime is four minutes.
